This `useEffect` exists only to mirror the Onyx value `lockedVBAUnlockRequestedNVPs` into a ref. That indirection is unnecessary — the other three call sites in this PR read the value directly. Reading it directly in the `paymentMethodPressed` handler keeps it always in sync and removes an extra effect (and the `useRef`).

Drop the ref and effect and read the value directly. Better yet, subscribe to the single relevant key so no lookup is needed:

```tsx
// eslint-disable-next-line rulesdir/no-default-id-values
const [unlockRequestedAt] = useOnyx(`${ONYXKEYS.COLLECTION.NVP_LOCKED_VBA_UNLOCK_REQUESTED}${accountData?.bankAccountID ?? CONST.DEFAULT_NUMBER_ID}`);
// ...then in the handler, use `unlockRequestedAt` directly
```

This mirrors the prop-derived `displayMerchantValue` into local `merchantInput` state and keeps them in sync with a `useEffect`. Syncing state to a prop in an effect runs a wasteful extra render pass: React first renders with the stale mirror, then the effect fires and triggers a second render with the corrected value. React's own guidance ("You Might Not Need an Effect") recommends adjusting the mirrored state *during render* by comparing the previous prop, rather than in an effect.

Replace the effect with a render-time adjustment so the mirror stays synced without an extra render, e.g.:

```tsx
const [merchantInput, setMerchantInput] = useState(displayMerchantValue);
const [prevDisplayValue, setPrevDisplayValue] = useState(displayMerchantValue);

// Adjust the mirror during render (not in an effect) when the persisted value changes while the field isn't focused
if (displayMerchantValue !== prevDisplayValue) {
    setPrevDisplayValue(displayMerchantValue);
    if (!isMerchantInputFocused.current) {
        setMerchantInput(displayMerchantValue);
    }
}
```

This keeps the same focus-gated behavior while avoiding the effect-driven double render.

This `useEffect` exists only to mirror the already-derived value `isInitialCreationFlow` into `isInitialCreationFlowRef.current`. `isInitialCreationFlow` is computed synchronously in the component body, so syncing it into a ref via an effect is redundant indirection — it adds an extra render pass and a second source of truth that can lag behind the derived value.

Drop the ref and the sync effect, and read the derived value directly in the cleanup by adding it to the existing cleanup effect's dependency array so the closure captures the latest value:

```tsx
// remove: isInitialCreationFlowRef and the sync useEffect

useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
        if (isHandingOffToInviteRef.current) {
            return;
        }
        clearInviteDraft(route.params.policyID);
        if (isInitialCreationFlow) {
            clearApprovalWorkflow();
        }
    };
}, [route.params.policyID, isInitialCreationFlow]);
```

This `useEffect` exists only to reset `manuallyCollapsedLinkedReportActionID` state in response to the `linkedReportActionID` prop changing. Resetting/adjusting state from a prop change inside an effect triggers an extra render pass and desynchronizes the derived `forceExpandedLinkedReportActionID` for one frame after the route changes (the stale collapse memory is still applied on the render before the effect fires).

Adjust the state during render using a previous-value tracker instead of an effect, so the correct value is available on the same render:

```ts
const [manuallyCollapsedLinkedReportActionID, setManuallyCollapsedLinkedReportActionID] = useState<string>();
const [prevLinkedReportActionID, setPrevLinkedReportActionID] = useState(linkedReportActionID);

if (linkedReportActionID !== prevLinkedReportActionID) {
    setPrevLinkedReportActionID(linkedReportActionID);
    if (manuallyCollapsedLinkedReportActionID !== undefined && manuallyCollapsedLinkedReportActionID !== linkedReportActionID) {
        setManuallyCollapsedLinkedReportActionID(undefined);
    }
}
```

This removes the effect (and its `eslint-disable`) and keeps the collapse memory consistent within the same render.

This `useEffect` has no dependency array, so it runs on every render purely to write a value derived from `personalBankAccount` into `flowRoutingDataRef`. It performs no external side effect — it just recomputes derived state — which is exactly the "you might not need an Effect" case: a derived value should be computed directly in the component body rather than synced into a ref via an effect that fires every render.

Compute the routing data inline and keep the ref updated during the same render path that guards `hasExitedFlowRef`, e.g.:

```tsx
const {onSuccessFallbackRoute, exitReportID} = personalBankAccount ?? {};
const flowRoutingData = !onSuccessFallbackRoute && !exitReportID ? undefined : {onSuccessFallbackRoute, exitReportID};
if (!hasExitedFlowRef.current) {
    flowRoutingDataRef.current = flowRoutingData;
}
```

This removes the extra effect while still capturing the latest routing data for the unmount cleanup.

This `useEffect` only mirrors `reportActions` into `reportActionsRef.current`. That is a pure synchronization with no side effect, and it needlessly defers the ref update to the commit phase (so the callback can read a stale value between render and effect). The sibling `reportAttributesRef` on the lines just above already does this correctly by assigning directly in the render body.

Remove the effect and assign the ref during render for consistency:

```tsx
const reportActionsRef = useRef(reportActions);
reportActionsRef.current = reportActions;
```

`existingFormValues` is computed inline on every render (line 80) via `getSpendRuleFormValuesFromCardRule(existingRule)`, producing a new object reference each time. Since it is listed as a `useEffect` dependency on line 96, the effect will re-fire on every render, repeatedly calling `setDraftSpendRule` with the same values. This risks an infinite render loop or unnecessary Onyx writes.

The previous code correctly computed `existingFormValues` inside the `useEffect`. To keep it outside (for the `useState` initializer), either memoize it or move the computation back inside the effect and extract a separate helper for the `useState` initializer:

```tsx
const [isRestrictMerchantsOff, setIsRestrictMerchantsOff] = useState(() => {
    const formValues = getSpendRuleFormValuesFromCardRule(existingRule);
    const hasNoMerchantRestrictions = !formValues?.merchantNames.length && !formValues?.categories?.length;
    return isNewRule || hasNoMerchantRestrictions;
});

useEffect(() => {
    if (!isEditingRule || !existingRule) {
        return;
    }
    const formValues = getSpendRuleFormValuesFromCardRule(existingRule);
    if (!formValues) {
        return;
    }
    setDraftSpendRule(formValues);
}, [existingRule, isEditingRule]);
```

Calling `setSelectedCardIDs(prunedSelectedCardIDs)` directly in the component body during render is a setState-during-render anti-pattern. It forces an unnecessary extra render cycle every time `filteredSortedCards` changes and the selection needs pruning. The pruned selection is a value that can be derived from existing state without updating it.

Instead of mutating state during render, derive the effective selection inline and use it throughout the component:

```tsx
const [selectedCardIDs, setSelectedCardIDs] = useState<number[]>([]);
const selectableCardIDs = filteredSortedCards.map((card) => card.cardID);

// Derive pruned selection - no setState needed
const effectiveSelectedCardIDs = selectedCardIDs.filter((id) => selectableCardIDs.includes(id));
```

Then replace all references to `selectedCardIDs` (in `isSelectAllChecked`, `isSelectAllIndeterminate`, `bulkExportOptions`, `getHeaderButtons`, `renderItem`, etc.) with `effectiveSelectedCardIDs`. This eliminates the extra render cycle and keeps the component idiomatic.

The `announcement` value is purely derived from `focusedIndex`, `maxLength`, and `translate` -- it can be computed directly in the component body instead of using a `useEffect` + `useState` pair. The current approach introduces an unnecessary extra render cycle: first `focusedIndex` changes and the component renders with the stale `announcement`, then the effect fires, calls `setAnnouncement`, and triggers a second render with the correct value.

Replace the `useState` + `useEffect` with a derived value:

```tsx
// Remove: const [announcement, setAnnouncement] = useState('');
// Remove: the useEffect that sets announcement

// Add: derive directly in the component body
const announcement = focusedIndex === undefined ? '' : translate('common.enterDigitLabel', {digitIndex: focusedIndex + 1, totalDigits: maxLength});
```

Both consumers (`useAccessibilityAnnouncement` and the hidden `<Text>` live region) will work identically with the derived value, since they only read the string.

