`setMergeTransactionKey(transactionID, mergeableData)` is a side effect (Onyx write via `Onyx.merge`) placed inside a `useMemo`. `useMemo` must be a pure computation with no side effects -- React may re-run it at any time, and in Strict Mode it runs twice during development. Deriving `conflictFields` via `useMemo` is correct, but the Onyx write should be moved to a separate `useEffect` that reacts to the computed data.

Extract the side effect into a `useEffect`:

```tsx
const {detectedConflictFields, mergeableData} = useMemo(() => {
    if (!transactionID || !targetTransaction || !sourceTransaction) {
        return {detectedConflictFields: [], mergeableData: {}};
    }
    return getMergeableDataAndConflictFields(
        targetTransaction, sourceTransaction, localeCompare,
        [targetTransactionReport, sourceTransactionReport],
        targetTransactionPolicy, sourceTransactionPolicy,
    );
}, [targetTransaction, sourceTransaction, transactionID, localeCompare, sourceTransactionReport, targetTransactionReport, targetTransactionPolicy, sourceTransactionPolicy]);

const conflictFields = detectedConflictFields as MergeFieldKey[];

useEffect(() => {
    if (transactionID && Object.keys(mergeableData).length > 0) {
        setMergeTransactionKey(transactionID, mergeableData);
    }
}, [transactionID, mergeableData]);
```

