The `updateTextInputFocusState` function uses `useCallback` but its sole purpose is to update a ref and conditionally clear another ref. Since refs are mutable and don't trigger re-renders, wrapping this in `useCallback` provides no performance benefit and adds unnecessary complexity.

**Suggested fix:** Remove the `useCallback` wrapper:

```tsx
const updateTextInputFocusState = (isTextInputFocused: boolean) => {
    isTextInputFocusedRef.current = isTextInputFocused;

    if (isTextInputFocused) {
        currentHoverIndexRef.current = null;
    }
};
```

This is simpler and has the same behavior since the function only updates refs (stable references).

