The `hasAppliedSavedMappings` ref is being used to track whether saved mappings have been applied, which could be derived from the state instead. This pattern unnecessarily complicates the component logic.

**Suggested fix:**

The condition `if (hasAppliedSavedMappings.current)` indicates this effect should only run once on mount when the dependencies are ready. Since the dependencies are `[existingCardID, spreadsheet?.data, savedColumnLayouts]`, you can compute whether mappings should be applied based on whether `spreadsheet?.columns` is already populated (by the saved mappings):

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
    if (\!existingCardID || \!spreadsheet?.data || \!savedColumnLayouts) {
        return;
    }

    const savedLayout = savedColumnLayouts[String(existingCardID)];
    if (\!savedLayout) {
        return;
    }

    // Only apply if columns haven't been set yet
    if (spreadsheet.columns && Object.keys(spreadsheet.columns).length > 0) {
        return;
    }

    applySavedColumnMappings(spreadsheet.data, savedLayout);
}, [existingCardID, spreadsheet?.data, spreadsheet?.columns, savedColumnLayouts]);
```

This removes the need for the ref and derives the state from props.

