The condition `selectedParticipants.some((participant) => participant.policyID === policy?.id)` can be computed directly during rendering instead of being used as a dependency in `useEffect`. This useEffect runs `setCustomUnitRateID` only when certain conditions are met, but the check itself could be derived inline.

**Suggested fix:** Consider extracting the condition as a derived value and using it in the effect guard:

```typescript
// Derive the check outside the effect
const hasParticipantWithPolicyID = useMemo(
    () => selectedParticipants.some((participant) => participant.policyID === policy?.id),
    [selectedParticipants, policy?.id]
);

useEffect(() => {
    if (
        \!["-1", CONST.CUSTOM_UNITS.FAKE_P2P_ID].includes(customUnitRateID) ||
        \!isDistanceRequest ||
        \!isPolicyExpenseChat ||
        \!transactionID ||
        \!lastSelectedRate ||
        isMovingTransactionFromTrackExpense ||
        \!hasParticipantWithPolicyID
    ) {
        return;
    }

    setCustomUnitRateID(transactionID, lastSelectedRate, transaction, policy);
}, [customUnitRateID, transactionID, lastSelectedRate, isDistanceRequest, isPolicyExpenseChat, isMovingTransactionFromTrackExpense, transaction, policy, hasParticipantWithPolicyID]);
```

This makes the derived logic explicit and easier to test.

